{{also|beissen|béissen|Beißen}}
==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|de|beyßen|beyssen||obsolete spelling}}
* {{alt|de|beissen||Switzerland|Liechtenstein|sometimes also Luxembourg|South Tyrol}}

===Etymology===
{{root|de|ine-pro|*bʰeyd-}} {{dercat|de|gmw-pro|gem-pro|ine-pro|inh=2}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|bīzen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|bīzan}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*bītan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*bītaną}}, from {{inh|de|ine-pro|*bʰéydt||to split}}.

Cognate with {{cog|nl|bijten}}, {{cog|nds|bieten}}, {{cog|en|bite}}, {{cog|da|bide}}, {{cog|sv|bita}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈbaɪ̯sən/|[ˈbäe̯sn̩]|}}
* {{audio|de|De-beißen.ogg}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|beißen<biss,gebissen>}}

# {{lb|de|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[bite]]
# {{lb|de|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[sting]]; to [[burn]]; to be [[sharp]]; to be [[spicy]]
# {{lb|de|reflexive|slightly|informal|of colors and clothes}} to [[clash]]; to [[jar]]

====Usage notes====
* In English, bloodsuckers are said to “bite”. In German, {{m|de||beißen}} is generally used with crawling bloodsuckers only, while {{m|de|stechen|t=to sting}} is used with flying bloodsuckers. Thus, you speak of a {{m|de|Flohbiss|t=flea bite}}, but a {{m|de|Mückenstich|t=mosquito bite}}.

